A1. This whistles at every station. ~ RADIO
A5. A reel queen. ~ GARBO
A9. Two of these means an exit. ~ TA
A10. While this means a lot to a farmer. ~ ACRE
A12. This will more than likely go to the dogs. ~ BONE
A13. What you live on besides your income (abbr.). ~ ST
A15. Barkers. ~ TREES
A16. To let out here and to take in there. ~ ALTER
A18. A Russian picture that is 100% silent. ~ ICON
A20. To lash with the food taster. ~ SCOLD
A22. You'll find this in the soup. ~ LEEK
A24. A black gob. ~ TAR
A25. This bird has a considerable reputation as a wise guy. ~ OWL
A26. To be, yet not to be. ~ ARE
A27. A poet's once. ~ ERST
A29. If left alone this will certainly rise to the top. ~ CREAM
A31. This has the map of Ireland all over its face. ~ ERIN
A32. Desired by the police. ~ SOUGHT
A34. Certain death. ~ DEMISE
A36. Often it's a stroke of luck to get away from this. ~ TEE
A37. A wiggly pole. ~ TAD
A38. A gentleman farmer. ~ YEOMAN
A41. Not present. ~ RECENT
A44. This kind of garden is bound to be wet. ~ BEER
A45. The bank that rarely fails. ~ PATER
A47. If you lose this you may never get home. ~ ROAD
A49. This is an addition to any home. ~ ELL
A50. Clean up by a dip. ~ RET
A51. This is brought up in an argument. ~ IRE
A52. This is stout and addicted to white collars (plur.). ~ ALES
A54. A peculiarity. ~ TRAIT
A56. Canned in Russia. ~ TSAR
A57. Juliet's mistake. ~ ROMEO
A59. Some one can be found to answer for this in France. ~ RENEE
A61. A noteworthy note. ~ TI
